What the numbers tell us
Depth at the high-threshold limit - 3 200 messages is exactly 80 % of the configured maximum. Because QDPHIEV is DISABLED, no depth-high alarm will be raised even though the queue is at its threshold.
All messages are uncommitted - UNCOM equals the current depth, meaning every message is waiting for a transaction commit. This blocks consumption and drives the depth up.
Very old messages - The oldest message has been sitting for about 4.5 days, indicating a significant backlog.
Limited parallelism - Only one consumer (IPPROCS = 1) and one producer (OPPROCS = 1) are attached, which can become a bottleneck if the workload is higher.

Recommended actions
Resolve the uncommitted transaction - Identify the application (producer or consumer) that left the transaction open and force it to commit or roll back. This will free the 3 200 messages for processing.
Enable depth-high events - Set QDPHIEV to ENABLED so the queue manager can alert when depth exceeds the 80 % threshold. Consider lowering the threshold for a critical queue.
Address the backlog - Add more consumer instances or increase IPPROCS (if the application design allows) so messages are consumed faster.
Review MAXDEPTH - If occasional spikes beyond 4 000 messages are expected, raise MAXDEPTH to give more headroom, but keep appropriate monitoring and alerts in place.
Check transaction handling - Look for long-running transactions that may be causing the high UNCOM count and shorten their windows where possible.
Continue regular monitoring - With MONQ already set to HIGH, use the collected metrics to track trends in CURDEPTH, UNCOM, and MSGAGE, and set up alerts for abnormal growth.

Bottom line
ACME.COMMERCE.QUEUE on QMS1 is sitting at its high-depth threshold, all messages are uncommitted, and the oldest messages are several days old. The most urgent step is to clear the pending transaction and enable depth-high alerts, then consider adding consumers or increasing MAXDEPTH to prevent future buildup.

